      "problem": "Which of the following plots shows the graph of $f(x)={(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}?$",
      "worked_solution": "The graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}$ has a $y$-intercept at $(0,1),$ grows rapidly for $x<0,$ and approaches the asymptote $y=0$ for $x>0.$\n\nWhen $x=-1,$ we have $y={(\\frac{1}{5})}^{-1}=5.$ Therefore, another point on the graph is $(-1,5).$ \n\nWe draw the graph through these points as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14194784/assets/1f1caf20957931c2453c07ac84d5a104d6e49951436e88d569e31bbe205b0244.png)",
